3.3(2)(A) (A) a description of the assessment tools and process used;
3.3(2)(B) (B) a description of the problem behaviors, including an definition for each target behavior;
3.3(2)(C) (C) prediction of the circumstances in which the problem behavior is most likely to occur;
3.3(2)(D) (D) identification of the function of the problem behavior;
3.3(2)(E) (E) assessment date;
3.3(2)(F) (F) name of the BC1 Staff completing the assessment;
3.3(2)(G) (G) name of others involved, if applicable; and
3.3(2)(H) (H) name of the Person being assessed.

3.4(1)(A) (A) a summary or hypothesis statement that clarifies the antecedent-behavior-consequence relationships describing the critical problem behavior, predicting the circumstances in which the problem behavior is most likely to occur, and identifying the function of the problem behavior;
3.4(1)(B) (B) baseline data with the date range the data was collected;
3.4(1)(C) (C) behavioral objectives written in measurable and observable terms;
3.4(1)(D) (D) data collection procedures that measure progress toward the BSP objectives that decrease problem behavior and increase replacement behavior;
3.4(1)(E) (E) clearly written and detailed behavioral intervention procedures to ensure consistent implementation by Staff and caregivers addressing the following areas:
3.4(1)(E)(i) (i) prevention procedures to decrease the likelihood of the problem behavior; (ii) planned responses for when the problem behavior occurs, including safety issues and efforts to minimize reinforcement of the problem behavior; (iii) teaching or increasing replacement behaviors; (iv) generalization of progress to other settings; and
3.4(1)(E)(v) (v) maintenance of progress and fading procedures.
3.4(1)(F) (F) name and title of the BC1 Staff who developed the BSP;
3.4(1)(G) (G) title of the Staff responsible for supervising the implementation of the BSP;
3.4(1)(H) (H) graphed data of the primary problem behaviors, updated at least every three;
3.4(1)(I) (I) provision for monitoring the implementation of the BSP;
3.4(1)(J) (J) provisions for training of family and Staff who provide supports to the Person; and
3.4(1)(K) (K) provisions for monthly evaluation of the effectiveness of the BSP and reassessment of the BSP as needed.

3.5(1)(A) (A) corporal punishment;
3.5(1)(B) (B) use speech to Persons that ridicules or is abusive;
3.5(1)(C) (C) locked confinement in a room;
3.5(1)(D) (D) use of electric devices or other painful stimuli to manage behavior;
3.5(1)(E) (E) denial or restriction of access to assistive technology, except where removal prevents injury to self, others, or property; and
3.5(1)(F) (F) withholding of meals as a consequence or punishment for problem behavior.

4.3(2)(A) (A) description of the assessment tools and process used;
4.3(2)(B) (B) description of the problem behaviors, including an operational definition for each target behavior;
4.3(2)(C) (C) prediction of the circumstances in which the problem behavior is most likely to occur;
4.3(2)(D) (D) identification of the function of the problem behavior;
4.3(2)(E) (E) date the assessment was conducted;
4.3(2)(F) (F) name of the BC2 Staff completing the assessment;
4.3(2)(G) (G) name of others involved, if applicable; and
4.3(2)(H) (H) name of the Person being assessed.

4.4(1)(A) (A) a summary or hypothesis statement that clarifies the antecedent-behavior-consequence relationships describing the critical problem behavior, predicting the circumstances in which the problem behavior is most likely to occur, and identifying the function of the problem behavior;
4.4(1)(B) (B) baseline data with the date range the data was collected;
4.4(1)(C) (C) behavioral objectives written in measurable and observable terms;
4.4(1)(D) (D) data collection procedures that measure progress toward the BSP objectives that decrease problem behavior and increase replacement behavior;
4.4(1)(E) (E) clearly written and detailed behavioral intervention procedures to ensure consistent implementation by Staff and caregivers addressing the following areas:
4.4(1)(E)(i) (i) prevention procedures designed to decrease the likelihood of the problem behavior; (ii) planned responses for when the problem behavior occurs, including safety issues and efforts to minimize reinforcement of the problem behavior; (iii) teaching or increasing replacement behaviors; (iv) generalization of progress to other settings; and
4.4(1)(E)(v) (v) maintenance of progress fading procedures.
4.4(1)(F) (F) name and title of the BC2 Staff who developed the BSP;
4.4(1)(G) (G) title of the Staff responsible for supervising the implementation of the BSP;
4.4(1)(H) (H) graphed data of the primary problem behaviors, updated at least every 3 months for visual analysis;
4.4(1)(I) (I) provision for monitoring the implementation of the BSP;
4.4(1)(J) (J) provisions for training of family and Staff who provide supports to the Person; and
4.4(1)(K) (K) provisions for monthly evaluation of the effectiveness of the BSP and reassessment of the BSP as needed.

4.5(1)(A) (A) corporal punishment, including slapping, hitting, or pinching;
4.5(1)(B) (B) demeaning speech to Persons that ridicules or is abusive;
4.5(1)(C) (C) locked confinement in a room;
4.5(1)(D) (D) use of electric devices or other painful stimuli to manage behavior;
4.5(1)(E) (E) denial or restriction of access to assistive technology, except where removal prevents injury to self, others, or property; and
4.5(1)(F) (F) withholding of meals as a consequence or punishment for problem behavior.

4.6(a)(1) (1) have at least one year of experience working with people with ID.RC or adults with ABI;
4.6(a)(2) (2) be currently certified through the Behavior Analyst Certification Board ("BACB") as an Assistant Behavior Analyst BCaBA; and
4.6(a)(3) (3) have a current Utah DOPL license as an LaBA.
4.6(b)(1) (1) have at least one year of experience working with people with ID.RC or adults with ABI;
4.6(b)(2) (2) have at least a master's degree in a behaviorally related field; and
4.6(b)(3) (3) have a current DOPL license as either:
4.6(c)(1) (1) have at least one year of experience working with people with ID.RC or adults with ABI;
4.6(c)(2) (2) be a student enrolled and actively engaged in a BACB approved program leading to certification as a BCaBA. The student shall:
4.6(c)(2)(A) (A) complete and pass all parts of the BCaBA approved course sequence;
4.6(c)(2)(B) (B) obtain all of the required supervision hours;
4.6(c)(2)(C) (C) apply for the first available BCaBA certification testing date offered by the BACB until the exam is passed;
4.6(c)(2)(D) (D) pass the BCaBA exam;
4.6(c)(2)(E) (E) obtain a BCaBA certification;
4.6(c)(2)(F) (F) obtain DOPL licensure as an LaBA; and
4.6(c)(2)(G) (G) be supervised by a BCBA who has a current DOPL Behavior Analyst ("LBA") license.
4.6(c)(3) (3) have a letter from their BCBA supervisor stating the supervisor's name and contact information and that they are willing to supervise the BC2 Staff.
4.6(d)(1) (1) have at least one year of experience working with people with ID.RC and/or adults with ABI;
4.6(d)(2) (2) have at least a master's degree in a behaviorally related field; and
4.6(d)(3) (3) be exempt from DOPL licensure as listed in Utah Code §58-61-707 Exemptions from licensure. The BC2 Staff shall have:
4.6(d)(3)(A) (A) a letter stating the reason for the exemption from DOPL licensure as outlined in Utah Code §58-61-707 Exemptions from Licensure; and
4.6(d)(3)(B) (B) the relevant DOPL licensure if required with the exemption.

